---
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';
import Ribbon from '../components/Ribbon.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { REPO_URL, href } from '../lib/data.ts';

const issues = `${REPO_URL}/issues`;
const policy = `${REPO_URL}/blob/main/docs/DATA_POLICY.md`;
---

<Base title="About" description="How ConAtlas TX verifies Texas anime convention dates.">
  <article class="about grid max-w-[72ch] gap-8">
    <div class="grid gap-3">
      <Ribbon rule>About</Ribbon>
      <h1 class="m-0 [font:var(--type-h1)] text-balance">
        A fan-made map of Texas anime conventions
      </h1>
      <p class="m-0 [font:var(--type-body-lg)] text-pretty text-text-2">
        ConAtlas TX exists because finding out which cons are happening, when, and whether the dates
        are real meant checking a dozen sites and a few group chats. We wanted one calm page that
        just tells you.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section id="texas">
      <h2>Texas only, on purpose</h2>
      <p>
        Keeping to one state means a small group of fans can actually visit every organizer's site
        and keep the list current. North, Central, Gulf Coast, South and West Texas are all covered.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="what-counts">
      <h2>What counts as an anime convention</h2>
      <p>
        A ticketed or free public event in Texas whose main programming is anime, manga or closely
        related fandom: artist alleys, cosplay, panels, screenings. General comic cons are listed
        only when the organizer describes them as an anime convention. Store meetups and private
        club nights are not.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="verify">
      <h2>How we verify dates</h2>
      <p>
        Every listing is checked against the organizer's own website and recorded in a public,
        version-controlled data file. A maintainer reviews every change before it's published. Each
        listing shows the date it was last checked and quotes the organizer's own words for every
        fact.
      </p>
      <p>
        When we can't confirm something we say so. Unannounced dates are labelled "Dates not
        announced" and never placed on a guessed weekend. The full rules are in our{' '}
        <a href={policy}>data policy</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="trust">
      <h2>Why you can trust it</h2>
      <p>
        Because you can see the source of every date, when it was checked, and the full change
        history in the open repository. If something's wrong, anyone can point it out and the fix is
        public too.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="contribute">
      <h2>How to contribute</h2>
      <p>
        Suggest a missing con or report a correction on the{' '}
        <a href={href('contribute/')}>Contribute page</a>, with a link to the organizer's own page.
        Code and data live on GitHub under open licenses.
      </p>
      <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2.5">
        <Button variant="secondary" href={REPO_URL} external>
          Source on GitHub
        </Button>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="license">
      <h2>Open source and data license</h2>
      <p>
        The website code is MIT licensed. The convention data files are released under CC BY 4.0:
        reuse them, with attribution to ConAtlas TX. Convention names and marks belong to their
        organizers; we list them, we don't represent them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="privacy">
      <h2>Privacy</h2>
      <p>
        No accounts, no tracking scripts, no cookies, and no requests to other sites: fonts are
        served from here. The only things stored are your light or dark mode choice and your saved
        list, both kept in your own browser. If we add analytics later it will be aggregate-only and
        documented here first.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="a11y">
      <h2>Accessibility</h2>
      <p>
        We aim for WCAG 2.2 AA: keyboard access everywhere, visible focus, status shown with words
        and icons rather than color alone, and a list view that works without the calendar grid.
        Every date and venue is plain HTML, so the site works without JavaScript. If something gets
        in your way, please <a href={issues}>tell us</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  section {
    display: grid;
    gap: 10px;
  }
  h2 {
    margin: 0;
    font: var(--type-h3);
  }
  section p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text-2);
  }
</style>
